  "_subjectComment": "How to pull company and role out of a subject line. Each entry is a regular expression with named groups; the first one that matches is used.",
  "subjectPatterns": [
    "^(?:Your )?[Aa]pplication (?:for|to) (?<role>.+?) at (?<company>.+?)$",
    "^(?<company>.+?): (?:Your )?application for (?<role>.+?)$",
    "^Thank you for applying to (?<role>.+?) at (?<company>.+?)$",
    "^(?:Update on your|Your) application (?:for|to) (?<role>.+?)(?: at (?<company>.+?))?$",
    "^Application received\\s*[:\u2013\u2014-]\\s*(?<role>.+?)(?: at (?<company>.+?))?$",
    "^(?<company>.+?) (?:has )?received your application(?: for (?<role>.+?))?$",
    "^Thank you for your (?:job )?application (?:to|with) (?<company>.+?)$",
    "^Your [Aa]pplication with (?<company>.+?)\\s+[\u2013\u2014-]\\s+.*$"
  ],
  "_ignoreComment": "Subjects only. There is deliberately no sender list: LinkedIn sends 'your application was sent to X' and 'Few&Far is hiring' from the same address, and Indeed sends employer decisions from the address its alerts come from. Blocking a sender threw away real mail three times. What an email IS gets decided by reading it \u2014 see the gate in extract.js.",
